{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Aaron spoke.</b> The term דִּבּוּר means nothing but a term of boldness, as it says ״וַיְדַבֵּר הָעָם״ etc. (Numbers 21). Is it possible that Moses was angry with Elazar and with Itamar, and Aaron speaks? You know that it was only out of respect; they said: It is not fitting for our father to sit and us to speak before him, and it is not fitting for a student to answer his master. Might one say it was because Elazar was not capable of answering? Scripture says ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֶלְעָזָר הַכֹּהֵן אֶל אַנְשֵׁי הַצָּבָא״ etc. (there, 31) — here, when he wished, he spoke before Moses and before the princes. This I found in a second inner book." "<b>Behold, today they offered.</b> What does it say? Rather, Moses said to him: Perhaps you sprinkled its blood while אוֹנְנִין — for does an אוֹנֵן who officiates render it profane? Aaron said to him: Did they offer it, that they are laymen? I brought it near, for I am the high priest, and an אוֹנֵן may officiate (Zevachim 101)." "<b>And if such things had befallen me.</b> Even if the dead were not my sons, but other relatives for whom I am obligated to be an אוֹנֵן — like all those mentioned in the priests' section for whom the priest becomes impure (Sifra)." "<b>And I ate the sin-offering.</b> And if I ate it — <b>would it have pleased, etc.</b>" "<b>Today.</b> But אֲנִינוּת at night is permitted, for the אוֹנֵן is only on the day of burial (Sifra; Zevachim 100)." "<b>Would it be pleasing in the eyes of YHWH.</b> If you heeded regarding the sanctuary-offerings of the moment, you have no permission to be lenient regarding the sanctuary-offerings for all generations."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:05:50.176837571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}